{"name":"Painter’s Canvas","hex":"#f9f2de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"painter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artist who paints pictures.","A laborer or workman who paints surfaces using a paintbrush or other means."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chain or rope used to attach the shank of an anchor to the side of a ship when not in use. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A rope that is attached to the bow of a dinghy, or other small boat, and used for tying up or towing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/painter"},{"term":"canvas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of coarse cloth, woven from hemp (traditionally) or from cotton and polyesters, useful for making sails, tents, and overcoats or as a surface for paintings.","A piece of such cloth stretched across a frame on which one may paint an artwork."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (an area or object) with canvas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canvas"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canvas","blocks":[{"text":"Canvas is a durable plain-woven fabric used for making sails, tents, marquees, backpacks, shelters, as a support for oil painting and for other items for which sturdiness is required, as well as in such fashion objects as handbags, electronic device cases, and shoes. It is popularly used by artists as a painting surface, typically stretched across a wooden frame."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg/330px-Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canvas","wikidata":"Q4259259"},{"title":"Painting","blocks":[{"text":"Painting is a visual art, characterized by the practice of applying paint, pigment, color, or other medium to a solid surface. The medium is commonly applied to the base with a brush. Other implements, such as palette knives, sponges, airbrushes, the artist's fingers, or even a dripping technique that uses gravity may be used. One who produces paintings is called a painter."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Mona_Lisa%2C_by_Leonardo_da_Vinci%2C_from_C2RMF_retouched.jpg/330px-Mona_Lisa%2C_by_Leonardo_da_Vinci%2C_from_C2RMF_retouched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Painting","wikidata":"Q11629"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Canvas roof at the Erasmus station of the Brussels Metro","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/500px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/1280px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG/500px-Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of Poland's biggest canvas paintings, the Battle of Grunwald, 1878, by Jan Matejko (426 cm × 987 cm (168 in × 389 in)), displayed in the National Museum in Warsaw[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitwa_pod_Grunwaldem_Muzeum_Narodowe_05.JPG"},{"srcset"